.TH GMM 1 "November 2026" "gmm 4.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gmm \- GHC C\-\- Manager: build tool and compiler driver for C\-\- projects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gmm
[\fIflags\fR] \fIinput.cmm\fR [\fIextra.o\fR|\fIlib.a\fR|\fIlib.so\fR ...]
.br
.B gmm
[\fIflags\fR] {\fBbuild\fR | \fB\-b\fR}
.br
.B gmm
[\fIflags\fR] {\fBbc\fR | \fB\-bc\fR}
.br
.B gmm
[\fIflags\fR] {\fBrun\fR | \fB\-r\fR} [\fIexecutable\fR]
.br
.B gmm
[\fIflags\fR] {\fBbr\fR | \fB\-br\fR}
.br
.B gmm
[\fIflags\fR] {\fBwatch\fR | \fB\-w\fR}
.br
.B gmm
[\fIflags\fR] {\fBclean\fR | \fB\-clean\fR}
.br
.B gmm
[\fIflags\fR] {\fBinit\fR | \fB\-init\fR}
.br
.B gmm
{\fBdoctor\fR | \fB\-doctor\fR}
.br
.B gmm
{\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-v\fR}
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gmm
(GHC C\-\- Manager) compiles C\-\- source files (\fI.cmm\fR) with the C\-\- front end
of the Glasgow Haskell Compiler and links the result into an executable or a
shared library.
It takes care of everything around the compiler call: it downloads and
configures a GHC toolchain on first use, extracts the bundled C\-\- headers and
support libraries, compiles any C sources that belong to the project, links
with the system C compiler (or with GHC itself), cleans up intermediate files,
and can run the resulting program or rebuild it whenever a source file changes.
.PP
A project can be described entirely on the command line, or in a small
configuration file named
.I conf.hmm
(see
.BR "CONFIGURATION FILE" ).
Values given on the command line take precedence over values in
.IR conf.hmm .
.PP
.B gmm
is written in Go and ships as a single binary.
The C\-\- support files it needs (the \fIlibgmm\fR directory) are embedded in the
binary and extracted to a temporary directory for each run.
.SH COMMANDS
A command is the first non\-flag argument.
Most commands also exist as a flag form (\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-r\fR, ...).
.TP
.BR build ", " b ", " \-b
Build the project described by
.I conf.hmm
(or by the input file given on the command line).
Fails if there is neither a configuration file nor an input file.
.TP
.BR bc ", " \-bc
Build the project and remove only the object files that
.B gmm
created during this run.
Files that existed before the run are never removed.
In version 4.2 intermediate objects are already deleted after every build
unless
.B \-keep\-obj
is given, so this command behaves like
.BR build .
.TP
.BR run ", " r ", " \-r ", " \-run " [\fIexecutable\fR]"
Without a build command: run an already built program and exit with its
status.
The program is the optional argument, otherwise the
.B output
from
.IR conf.hmm ,
otherwise
.IR a.out .
A bare file name without a directory part is run as
.IR ./name .
Combined with a build command (for example
.BR "gmm \-b \-r" ),
the program is started after a successful build.
.TP
.BR br ", " \-br
Build using
.I conf.hmm
and run the program immediately on success.
.TP
.BR watch ", " w ", " \-w ", " \-watch
Build, then rebuild every time the watched files change, until
.B Ctrl\-C
is pressed.
Add
.B \-r
to run the program after every successful build.
A failed build is reported and the watcher keeps waiting for the next change.
See
.BR "WATCH MODE" .
.TP
.BR clean ", " \-clean
Remove the files
.B gmm
produces for this project: the object files, the output file, and the
assembly file if assembly output is enabled.
Source files, C sources and user supplied objects are never touched.
With
.B \-n
it only prints what would be removed.
.TP
.BR init ", " \-init
Write a new
.I conf.hmm
in the current directory from the current command line.
The input defaults to
.I main.cmm
when none is given.
An existing file is not overwritten unless
.B \-f
is given.
With
.B \-n
the generated text is printed instead of written.
.TP
.BR doctor ", " \-doctor
Check the toolchain and the environment and exit.
See
.BR DOCTOR .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Set the output file name, for example
.BR "\-o app" .
.TP
.BI \-o " C=\(dq<target>\(dq"
Select the output format (see
.BR TARGETS ).
Both forms can be combined and repeated:
.BR "gmm \-o C=\(dqso\(dq \-o libapp main.cmm" .
A bare
.BI C= target
argument is also accepted among the positional arguments.
.TP
.BI \-I " path"
Add an include directory for C and C\-\- headers.
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-D " name" [= value ]
Define a macro for the C\-\- preprocessor.
May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-S
Emit assembly (\fI.s\fR) instead of building an executable or library.
.TP
.BI \-libs " list"
Comma\-separated list of additional libraries to link.
May be repeated.
A plain name such as
.B m
becomes
.BR \-lm ;
an entry that starts with
.BR \- ,
contains a path separator, or ends in
.IR .so ,
.IR .a ,
.I .dll
or
.I .lib
is passed to the linker unchanged.
.TP
.B \-keep\-obj
Keep intermediate object files after linking.
.TP
.B \-llvm
Compile with the GHC LLVM backend (\fB\-fllvm\fR).
Needs an LLVM
.B opt
tool in the version range accepted by GHC 9.12 (LLVM 13 to 20).
.TP
.B \-rts
Use GHC as the linker driver (with \fB\-no\-hs\-main\fR) instead of the system
C compiler.
.TP
.BI \-opt " 0|1|2"
Optimisation level handed to GHC (\fB\-O0\fR, \fB\-O1\fR, \fB\-O2\fR).
The forms
.BR 2 ,
.B O2
and
.B \-O2
are equivalent.
.TP
.BI \-ghc\-opt " flags"
Extra flags passed straight to GHC, comma\-separated.
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-ld\-opt " flags"
Extra flags passed to the linker command, comma\-separated.
May be repeated.
See
.B NOTES
about flags that contain a comma.
.TP
.B \-strip
Strip symbols from the linked output.
.TP
.BI \-arg " value"
Argument given to the program started by
.B \-r
or
.BR \-br .
May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-watch
Rebuild when the project changes.
.TP
.B \-clean
Same as the
.B clean
command.
.TP
.B \-init
Same as the
.B init
command.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-force
Let
.B init
overwrite an existing
.IR conf.hmm .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-dry\-run
Print the commands that would run without running anything.
A dry run never downloads the toolchain; if GHC is not installed it stops with
an error that asks for one normal run first.
.TP
.B \-time
Print how long each build phase took (compile, C files, link, ...).
.TP
.B \-verbose
Echo every external command before it runs.
.TP
.B \-doctor
Check the toolchain and environment, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-version
Print the version, developer and repository, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-help
Print the built\-in help text and exit.
.SH TARGETS
The target is chosen with
.B \-o C=\(dq<target>\(dq
or with the
.B target
key of
.IR conf.hmm .
Each name matches the file that is produced.
.TP
.B native
Plain executable for the current host. This is the default.
The output is named
.I a.out
unless a name is given (\fI.exe\fR is appended on Windows).
Alias:
.BR host .
.TP
.B exe
Windows PE executable; the output gets the
.I .exe
suffix.
Aliases:
.BR windows ,
.BR pe .
.TP
.B dll
Windows PE shared library; the output gets the
.I .dll
suffix.
.TP
.B linux
Linux ELF executable; the output gets the
.I .elf
suffix when the name has no extension.
Alias:
.BR elf .
.TP
.B so
Native shared object; the output gets the
.I .so
suffix (a name that already ends in
.I .so
or
.I .dylib
is kept).
Alias:
.BR shared .
.PP
When no output name is given for
.BR exe ,
.BR dll ,
.B linux
or
.BR so ,
the base name of the input file is used with the matching suffix.
For example
.B gmm \-o C=\(dqso\(dq main.cmm
writes
.IR main.so ,
and
.B gmm \-o C=\(dqso\(dq \-o app main.cmm
writes
.IR app.so .
.PP
.B gmm
does not cross\-compile.
A target that belongs to another operating system than the host (for example
.B exe
or
.B dll
on Linux, or
.B linux
on Windows) is refused with an error.
Use
.B native
for a plain executable for the host.
Shared objects and DLLs are not run by
.B \-r
(a warning is printed instead).
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.B gmm
reads
.I conf.hmm
from the directory of the input file, then from the current directory; the
first one found is used.
The format is one
.I key=value
per line.
Blank lines and lines that start with
.B #
or
.B ;
are ignored.
Values may be quoted.
Keys are case\-insensitive and unknown keys are ignored, so newer files stay
loadable by older versions.
A line without
.B =
is an error that names the file and line number.
.PP
Lists are comma\-separated.
Booleans accept
.BR 1 / true / yes / on
and
.BR 0 / false / no / off .
A boolean key can only switch an option on; an option already enabled on the
command line is never switched off by
.BR false .
.TP
.BR input " (" src ", " source )
The C\-\- source file.
.TP
.BR output " (" out ", " bin )
Output name. A command line
.B \-o
wins.
.TP
.BR target " (" type ", " format )
One of
.BR native ,
.BR exe ,
.BR dll ,
.BR linux ,
.BR so .
The suffix implied by the target is added to the output name.
.TP
.BR includes " (" incdirs )
Include directories.
.TP
.BR defines " (" defs )
Macros, as
.B NAME
or
.BR NAME=value .
.TP
.BR cfiles " (" csources ", " csrc )
C source files compiled with the C compiler and linked in.
Each
.I x.c
becomes
.IR x_c.o .
.TP
.BR libs " (" libraries )
Libraries to link, same rules as
.BR \-libs .
.TP
.BR objs " (" objects ", " extraobjs )
Extra object files or archives to link.
.TP
.BR keepobj " (" keep\-obj )
Keep intermediate objects.
.TP
.BR emitasm " (" asm )
Emit assembly instead of building.
.TP
.B llvm
Use the LLVM backend.
.TP
.B rts
Link with GHC instead of the C compiler.
.TP
.B run
Run the program after building.
.TP
.BR opt " (" optimize )
Optimisation level: 0, 1 or 2.
.TP
.B strip
Strip symbols.
.TP
.BR args " (" runargs )
Arguments for the program started by run. Arguments given with
.B \-arg
win.
.TP
.BR ghcopts " (" ghc\-opts )
Extra GHC flags.
.TP
.BR ldopts " (" ld\-opts )
Extra linker flags.
.PP
Include directories, macros and libraries from the command line are placed
before the ones from
.IR conf.hmm .
The
.B init
command writes a file with exactly these keys from the current command line.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# conf.hmm generated by gmm 4.2
input=main.cmm
output=app
target=linux
opt=2
libs=m,pthread
includes=include
defines=DEBUG,VERSION=3
cfiles=helpers.c
args=--verbose,input.txt
.RE
.fi
.SH POSITIONAL FILE ARGUMENTS
Besides the input file, extra positional arguments are classified by suffix:
.IR .so ,
.IR .a ,
.I .dll
and
.I .lib
files are linked as libraries; everything else is treated as an extra object
file.
The input is the first argument that ends in
.IR .cmm ;
if none does, the first argument is used and a warning about the missing
.I .cmm
extension is printed.
.SH BUILD PROCESS
A normal build performs these steps, in order:
.IP 1. 4
Locate or install the toolchain (see
.BR TOOLCHAIN ).
.IP 2. 4
Extract the embedded
.I libgmm
files to a temporary directory.
That directory is added to the include path, so the bundled headers can be
used with
.BR #include .
It is removed when
.B gmm
exits.
.IP 3. 4
Compile the C\-\- file with GHC:
.B \-c \-no\-hs\-main
plus
.B \-fllvm
(with
.BR \-llvm ),
.B \-O\fIn\fR
(with
.BR \-opt ),
.B \-fPIC
(for
.B so
and
.BR dll ),
include directories, macros (passed as
.BR "\-optc \-D..." ),
and any
.BR \-ghc\-opt
flags.
The object is written next to the input as
.IR name.o .
.IP 4. 4
Compile each C file from
.B cfiles
with the C compiler (with
.B \-fPIC
for shared libraries).
.IP 5. 4
If the C\-\- source mentions
.I cmmath.h
or
.IR cmmath.c ,
build the bundled math support object
.I cmmath.o
(\fIcmmath_lib.o\fR when the input itself is named
.IR cmmath.cmm )
and link it in.
.IP 6. 4
Link with the C compiler:
.B \-shared \-fPIC
for
.B so
and
.BR dll ,
.B \-no\-pie
for executables,
.BR \-g ,
the requested libraries,
.B \-lm \-lc
on non\-Windows systems,
.B \-s
with
.BR \-strip ,
and finally any
.B \-ld\-opt
flags.
With
.B \-rts
the link is done by GHC instead.
.IP 7. 4
Delete the intermediate object files, unless
.B \-keep\-obj
was given.
A failed build never leaves half\-written objects behind.
.PP
With
.B \-S
only the assembly step is done and the result is written to
.I name.s
(or to the file given with
.BR \-o ).
.SH WATCH MODE
Watch mode fingerprints the input file, the quoted
.B #include
files that exist on disk (followed recursively), the C sources,
.IR conf.hmm ,
and rebuilds when any modification time or size changes.
It polls every half second and waits a moment after a change so editors can
finish writing.
.B Ctrl\-C
stops the watcher and exits with status 0.
.SH TOOLCHAIN
.B gmm
needs a GHC and a C compiler.
They are located in this order:
.IP 1. 4
.B GMM_GHC
in the environment (with
.B GMM_CC
and
.B GMM_LLVM
if set).
If it does not point to an executable file, gmm stops with an error instead of
falling back to another toolchain.
.IP 2. 4
A
.I sit.txt
file in an installation directory, with the lines
.BR GHC= ,
.B CC=
and
.BR LLVM= .
.IP 3. 4
A
.I Ghc/
directory inside an installation directory, which is searched for a GHC
binary and a C compiler (clang, gcc or cc next to GHC, then the system ones).
A
.I sit.txt
is written when one is found.
.IP 4. 4
Otherwise the toolchain is downloaded and unpacked.
.PP
The installation directories are
.B GMM_HOME
if set; otherwise on Linux and macOS
.I /usr/local/bin/gmm
and then
.IR ~/.local/share/gmm ;
on Windows
.IR "%ProgramFiles(x86)%\\\\gmm" .
The first writable one is used for a new installation.
.PP
The automatic download fetches GHC 9.12.4 for the host platform (Windows
x86_64, Linux x86_64, Linux aarch64, macOS arm64) from
.IR downloads.haskell.org ,
showing a progress bar, and unpacks it into
.IR Ghc/ .
No download is published for Windows arm64 or macOS x86_64; on those systems
install GHC yourself and set
.BR GMM_GHC .
.SH DOCTOR
.B gmm doctor
prints a report and exits with status 1 if a required tool is missing:
.IP \(bu 3
the gmm version and the host platform;
.IP \(bu 3
the GHC, C compiler and (optional) LLVM tools, with their paths, versions and
an OK or FAIL marker, or a note that the toolchain will be downloaded on the
first build;
.IP \(bu 3
the environment variables and installation directories;
.IP \(bu 3
the linkers available (\fBld.gold\fR, \fBld.bfd\fR, \fBld.lld\fR) on non\-Windows systems;
.IP \(bu 3
which targets are supported on this host;
.IP \(bu 3
the number of embedded library files.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GMM_HOME
Installation directory that holds
.I Ghc/
and
.IR sit.txt .
Overrides the default installation directories.
.TP
.B GMM_GHC
Path to a
.B ghc
binary. Skips the toolchain lookup.
.TP
.B GMM_CC
Path to the C compiler driver used for assembling and linking.
.TP
.B GMM_LLVM
Path to the LLVM
.B opt
tool, used with
.BR \-llvm .
If unset,
.B opt
is looked up in
.BR PATH .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I conf.hmm
Project configuration.
.TP
.I sit.txt
Remembered toolchain paths, inside the installation directory.
.TP
.I Ghc/
The downloaded GHC toolchain, inside the installation directory.
.TP
.I name.o, name_c.o, cmmath.o
Intermediate objects, written next to the sources and removed after linking.
.TP
.I a.out, name.exe, name.elf, name.so, name.dll
Default output names.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. (In watch mode, also after
.BR Ctrl\-C .)
.TP
.B 1
Build, link or configuration error, a failed
.B doctor
check, or
.B init
refusing to overwrite.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line.
.TP
.B 127
.B run
could not find the program to execute.
.PP
When
.B gmm
runs the program with
.BR \-r ,
it returns that program's own exit status.
.B Ctrl\-C
during a run is delivered to the program, not to
.BR gmm .
.SH EXAMPLES
Build an executable for this host:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm \-o app main.cmm
.RE
.PP
Build and run with an argument:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm \-r \-arg hello main.cmm
.RE
.PP
Optimised Linux ELF executable with the math library, stripped:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm \-o C=\(dqlinux\(dq \-o app \-opt 2 \-libs m \-strip main.cmm
.RE
.PP
Shared object linked with a C helper file and an extra archive:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm \-o C=\(dqso\(dq \-o libapp main.cmm helper.o libextra.a
.RE
.PP
Generate a project file, then build and run from it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fBgmm \-init \-o app \-opt 2 \-libs m main.cmm\fR
\fBgmm \-br\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rebuild and rerun on every change:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm \-w \-r
.RE
.PP
Show the commands and timings without hiding anything:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm \-verbose \-time main.cmm
.RE
.PP
See what a build would do, without doing it:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm \-n main.cmm
.RE
.PP
Remove everything the project produced:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm clean
.RE
.SH NOTES
.SS Flags that contain a comma
The list options
.BR \-ghc\-opt ,
.BR \-ld\-opt
and
.B \-libs
split their value at commas, and there is no escape for a comma.
A flag such as
.B \-Wl,\-Bsymbolic
therefore reaches the C compiler as two broken pieces.
Pass linker flags with
.B \-Xlinker
instead, one option per use:
.PP
.RS
.B gmm \-ld\-opt=\-Xlinker \-ld\-opt=\-Bsymbolic \-o C=\(dqso\(dq \-o app main.cmm
.RE
.SS Shared objects and position independent code
The native code generator of GHC may emit position\-dependent references to
symbols defined in the same C\-\- file (for example a string label), which the
linker rejects when building a shared object:
.PP
.RS
relocation R_X86_64_PC32 against symbol \(oqfmt\(cq can not be used when making a shared object
.RE
.PP
Two ways around it: build with the LLVM backend
.RB ( "\-llvm" ),
or let the linker bind such symbols locally with
.BR "\-ld\-opt=\-Xlinker \-ld\-opt=\-Bsymbolic" .
The latter means functions in the library cannot be overridden by another
library loaded earlier.
.SS Cross compilation
Targets for another operating system are rejected.
A Windows\-hosted GHC cannot be run on Linux directly; a Windows executable
from Linux needs a Windows\-targeting GHC (set
.BR GMM_GHC )
and a way to run it, such as Wine.
